My verdict is FAIL. The group-kill fix works and is well tested, but it causes a new problem in interactive sessions, and I've written the verdict to session_plan/eval_task_1.md.

The problem: the diff gives bash its own process group, but bash still uses yoyo's terminal as its input. When a process outside the terminal's foreground group tries to read from the terminal, the system pauses it. So in an interactive session, any model command that reads input or prompts now freezes silently until the timeout kills it. Examples are sudo, ssh, git credential prompts and read. Before this change, the user could answer those prompts.

Evidence:
• yoagent 0.25.2's BashTool, which the diff says it copies, does the same process-group setup but also gives the child no input (cmd.stdin(Stdio::null()), src/tools/bash.rs:190-192). The diff left that part out, and neither the code nor ARCHITECTURE.md mentions it.
• I reproduced the freeze in a pseudo-terminal: a child in its own group with terminal input, running read x, sat paused (process state T).

What is correct:
• `bash gets its own group. On timeout or cancel, the whole group is killed with SIGKILL before bash` itself is killed and reaped.
• The group is not killed when a command finishes on its own, and a dropped future still cleans up.
• The tests use unique sleep values, the "normal exit" test compares output exactly, and the ARCHITECTURE.md entry is thorough.
• Every new definition is used, the new test module is registered in src/main.rs, and no sabotage markers are left.

Fix: add cmd.stdin(std::process::Stdio::null()) next to the existing stdout/stderr pipe lines, plus a test. Alternatively, keep terminal input on purpose and write down why.
